#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/difficulty 1.313241991049613 :topic/id #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050400}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"}]}
